1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,105
上空から斜めへ視点を変えます。
Begin overhead, then tilt.

2
00:00:04,105 --> 00:00:11,074
機能を上下に重ねると、一部のダイ間経路を短縮できます。
Functions can be stacked rather than placed side by side, shortening selected die-to-die routes.

3
00:00:11,074 --> 00:00:18,657
回路基板を積むのではなく、管理された界面でダイを接合します。
This is not a stack of circuit boards. Dies meet at carefully controlled interfaces.

4
00:00:18,657 --> 00:00:30,000
層を数え、それぞれの役割を確認しましょう。
Count the layers and identify what each layer contributes before inspecting the bond.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,938
上下のダイは演算、記憶、接続などを分担し、同じ製造プロセスとは限りません。
Upper and lower dies can divide compute, memory and connection functions in different ways. Their process technologies need not match.

6
00:00:39,938 --> 00:00:50,216
この模型は一般的な教材で、特定の SoIC や Foveros 製品の内部配置ではありません。
This is a generic teaching model, not an internal floorplan of a particular SoIC or Foveros product.

7
00:00:50,216 --> 00:01:00,000
何を重ねるかはアーキテクチャで決めます。
Architecture determines which functions belong together.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,960
界面では銅接点が電気をつなぎ、周囲の絶縁体表面も接合します。
Approach the interface: copper contacts provide electrical connections while surrounding dielectric surfaces also bond.

9
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:13,063
この組合せがハイブリッド接合です。
That combination defines hybrid bonding.

10
00:01:13,063 --> 00:01:17,011
大きなはんだボールの列ではありません。
Do not picture a row of large solder balls.

11
00:01:17,011 --> 00:01:30,000
微細はんだバンプと直接接合は異なる方式で、制約も違います。
Solder microbumps and direct hybrid bonding are different interconnect approaches with different integration constraints.

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,269
ダイを離し、粒子や凹凸が界面に残る様子を考えます。
Separate the dies and imagine a particle or uneven surface trapped between them.

13
00:01:37,269 --> 00:01:45,419
表面処理、清浄度、平坦度、位置精度が接合品質を左右します。
Surface preparation, cleanliness, flatness and alignment work together to determine bond quality.

14
00:01:45,419 --> 00:02:00,000
動作を遅くして原理を示しており、洗浄レシピや工程温度を提供するものではありません。
The motion is slowed to explain the principle; it is not a cleaning recipe or a manufacturing temperature schedule.

15
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,706
接合界面は短くても、データは各ダイ内の回路や配線を通ります。
The bond interface is short, but data still traverses circuits and wiring within each die.

16
00:02:08,706 --> 00:02:18,652
縦方向接続が解決するのは経路の一部であり、論理遅延が消えるわけではありません。
Vertical connectivity solves only part of the path; it does not remove logic delay.

17
00:02:18,652 --> 00:02:30,000
界面を越える箇所を、信号経路全体の中で見直します。
Identify the crossing between dies, then place that crossing back into the complete signal chain.

18
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,424
高密度接点は層間通信を豊かにできますが、活用できる設計が必要です。
Dense contacts can support richer communication between layers, but the architecture must make use of them.

19
00:02:39,424 --> 00:02:46,173
接点や給電を決める前に、データと処理の分担を定めます。
Define the division of data and work before allocating contacts and power.

20
00:02:46,173 --> 00:03:00,000
接点の多さ自体が目的ではなく、光の動きも特定の転送速度を示しません。
More connections are not a goal by themselves. Animated movement shows information relationships, not a claimed transfer speed.

21
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,022
側面から熱の出口を追います。
From the side, follow heat out of the stack.

22
00:03:04,022 --> 00:03:12,019
発熱位置、界面熱抵抗、冷却器の位置が温度に影響します。
Heat-source location, interface resistance and cooler placement jointly affect temperature.

23
00:03:12,019 --> 00:03:15,621
高く積めばよいわけではありません。
A taller stack is not automatically better.

24
00:03:15,621 --> 00:03:30,000
最も熱い回路の配置は、組立後ではなく設計の初期に決める問題です。
Where the hottest circuits belong is an architectural decision to make early, not a problem to postpone until assembly.

25
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:39,427
他のダイに覆われた接点へは直接触れにくくなるため、試験経路を先に設計します。
Contacts become difficult to reach after another die covers them, so test access must be designed in advance.

26
00:03:39,427 --> 00:03:47,548
組立前の選別、接合後の導通、システム試験は役割が異なります。
Pre-assembly screening, post-bond connectivity and system testing serve different purposes.

27
00:03:47,548 --> 00:04:00,000
一度の合格は永続的保証ではなく、範囲と残るリスクを示します。
One passing test is not a permanent guarantee; coverage and remaining risk must be explicit.

28
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,684
全体を見ると、高密度化ほど界面品質と試験計画が重要です。
Zoom out: dense integration makes interface quality and test planning more important.

29
00:04:07,684 --> 00:04:16,071
接合後のダイ交換は経済的でない場合があり、事前選別の価値が高まります。
Bonded dies may not be economical to replace, increasing the value of earlier screening.

30
00:04:16,071 --> 00:04:30,000
接続密度だけでなく、良品単価、熱条件、修復性を一緒に比較します。
Compare solutions using cost per good unit, thermal conditions and repair options together, rather than connection density alone.

31
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:41,743
機能分割、界面設計、表面準備、位置合わせ、接合、試験性、熱検証が道筋です。
The route is functional partitioning, interface design, surface preparation, alignment, bonding, testability and thermal verification.

32
00:04:41,743 --> 00:04:46,029
3D の目的は高さではありません。
The aim of 3D is not merely a taller building.

33
00:04:46,029 --> 00:05:00,000
通信する機能を近づけつつ、電流、熱、試験の確かな通り道を残すことです。
It is to bring communicating functions closer while preserving dependable paths for electricity, heat and testing.
